RED audio: hand the opus decoder the primary block, not the whole data area.

0016 stripped the fixed five byte RED wrapper and then passed payload+5 .. end
to opus.  That slice starts with the **redundant block's payload**, not with
the primary block -- RFC 2198 lays the data area out as
[redundant1][redundant2]...[primary].  So the decoder kept receiving
"redundant + primary" glued together as a single frame:

  payload                        hdr  block1  primary   0016 gave  correct
  ef 0f 00 03 6f fc ff fe fc..   (5)   (3)      (3)        6         3
  ef 0f 00 a1 6f fc ..           (5)   (161)    (161)      322       161
  ef 0f 01 0b 6f fc ..           (5)   (267)    (215)      482       215
  ef 0f 00 d7 6f fc ..           (5)   (215)    (161)      376       161

CELT allows a 20 ms frame up to 1275 bytes, so opus never complained: it took
322 bytes as one frame and decoded it.  Frame counts, duration and loss
statistics all looked right -- only the content was wrong: the amplitude shot
to 1.38 and audio-graph's clampf(-1, 1) chopped it into a square wave.  On the
console that is "a cave, with the melody barely audible", while every counter
says "decoding is fine, errors=1".

What
----
  * rtp_red5_strip() becomes a real RFC 2198 chain walk: consume the block
    header area (an F=1 block is 4 bytes -- 14 bit timestamp offset + 10 bit
    length; the F=0 block is the primary, one header byte), sum the redundant
    lengths, and start the primary at hdr + sum(length).
  * Only payload type 63 is touched.  0016 keyed on payload[0] == 0xef, but a
    bare opus TOC (0xfc) has the F bit set too -- a wrong guess cuts a good
    packet in half.  pt != 63 now passes through byte for byte, so the bare
    opus path is exactly what it was before 0016.
  * A redundant block must be PT 111 (opus) and there may be at most 4 of
    them; anything else is forwarded untouched.  An unrecognised shape keeps
    the old "cannot decode" behaviour; mangling a packet that does decode is
    not acceptable.
  * The primary TOC is still shape-checked (frame count code 3 requires a
    following count byte holding 1..3).
  * The diagnostic prints the payload type: "[red5] pt=%d in=%d out=%d stripped=%d".

The redundant block is genuinely useful -- it is a free retransmit of the
previous 20 ms -- but using it needs a jitter buffer and a "this sequence
number really did not arrive" verdict.  That is 0018; nothing is guessed here.

Patch 0017 of the libpeer-switch series, applied after 0016-red-audio-depacket.patch
by tools/apply-libpeer-switch-patches.sh.


